\begin{document}

\title{Maxwell--Jones Vector-Sensor Localization and Synchronization for
Distributed Near-Field RIS-Aided OFDM}

\author{Qingshan Gong,~\IEEEmembership{Member,~IEEE}}

\markboth{IEEE Transactions on Wireless Communications,~Vol.~XX, No.~XX, 2026}%
{Gong: Maxwell--Jones Vector-Sensor Localization and Synchronization}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study joint 3-D localization and synchronization of a single-antenna user 
in a wideband orthogonal frequency-division multiplexing (OFDM) uplink in 
which the direct path is blocked and a single electromagnetic vector-sensor (EVS) 
array observes the user only through distributed near-field reconfigurable intelligent surfaces (RISs). 
All cascaded paths share an unknown clock offset, with unknown gains, Jones coefficients 
and panel associations. We propose a two-phase estimator built on a per-panel 
space--frequency/Maxwell--Jones factorization. Phase~I (MKSC-GI) applies a calibrated sufficient Maxwell--Kronecker compression that reduces the receive dimension from $96$ to $6$, recovers the delay factors and their 
panel associations, and fuses the panel-local near-field estimates into a common position initializer. 
Phase~II (CCOP-JVP) eliminates the Jones coefficients by variable projection and profiles the 
one-dimensional clock orbit by certified branch-and-bound, leaving a three-dimensional position refinement. 
The same factorization yields three structural results: receiver selection rescales single-panel free-Jones 
information only through the retained polarization energy, though two or fewer retained components can void 
panel separability; the compression preserves the profiled optimizer and the free-Jones information; 
and the clock profile is a finite trigonometric polynomial. At $-10$~dB SNR the estimator achieves 
a $0.31$-mm median position error with $0.42\%$ position and $0.21$ clock catastrophic-event rates, 
while the closest in-model 4-D control has comparable median accuracy but has a $1.98\%$ position catastrophic rate.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
RIS, EVS, near-field localization, clock synchronization, OFDM, polarimetric
sensing, variable projection, branch-and-bound, sufficient statistics, Fisher information.
\end{IEEEkeywords}


\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

\IEEEPARstart{R}{econfigurable} intelligent surfaces (RISs) make the
propagation environment itself programmable, and are studied as a low-cost
way to shape coverage in future wireless systems~\cite{wymeersch2020risloc}.
As their apertures grow electrically large at millimeter-wave and
sub-terahertz frequencies, the users they serve fall inside the radiative
near field, where the plane-wave approximation no longer holds and the
curvature of the wavefront across the aperture carries range in addition to
direction~\cite{cui2023nearfield}. That extra geometric
content turns an electrically large surface from a communication device into
a passive anchor, able to fix a user in three dimensions without active
infrastructure of its own~\cite{elzanaty2021bounds}. Deploying several such
panels adds spatially diverse reflected paths, which matters most when the
direct link to the base station (BS) is obstructed. A passive RIS provides
no independent timing reference, however, so the delays measured through such
a deployment carry
the clock offset of the user equipment (UE) as well as its position, and the
two have to be recovered together. This joint problem lies at the
intersection of three largely separate lines of work: RIS-aided
localization and synchronization, near-field and
extremely-large-aperture estimation, and polarimetric sensing.

The first line, RIS-aided localization and synchronization, has moved from
single-panel geometry to distributed
deployments~\cite{ma2023distributed,liu2025multiris} and to formulations
that carry an unknown clock
explicitly~\cite{fascista2022rissync,gong2024async,peng2025sdp,yan2025ris}.
The second line is near-field and
extremely-large-aperture estimation, which supplies both the spherical-wave
models this regime needs and the acquisition front ends that make them
tractable: multilinear factorization of the training
tensor~\cite{lin2021tensor}, sparse and variational recovery on a
discretized manifold~\cite{li2024vbi}, and polar or beam-split codebooks
matched to wavefront
curvature~\cite{cui2022channel,yang2024nfris,zheng2025wideband}.
The third line is polarimetric sensing, where polarization enters RIS work
mainly as a controllable resource -- dual-polarized surfaces spend it on
beamforming gain~\cite{han2022dual} -- whereas array
processing treats it as an unknown to be estimated: an electromagnetic vector
sensor (EVS) measures co-located electric and magnetic fields and so resolves
the polarization state of an incident wave alongside its
direction~\cite{nehorai1994vector,khan2020sixcomponent}. Our own EVS-OFDM
(orthogonal frequency-division multiplexing)
estimator~\cite{gong2026aimdf} applies such an array to a direct multipath
channel, with none of the distributed cascaded geometry, panel labeling,
compression or shared-clock structure at issue here.

Despite these advances, the resulting estimation problem remains unresolved
because the limitations of the three lines compound in this architecture.
First, distributed RIS localization and synchronization typically resolve
panel identities before or during the joint
fit~\cite{ma2023distributed,liu2025multiris,fascista2022rissync}. Here, all
cascades reach the same EVS array within one observation and an algebraic
front end returns them unlabeled~\cite{lin2021tensor}; two panels at nearly
coincident cascaded delays present increasingly similar space--frequency
evidence. Their separability then also depends on the retained Maxwell
structure of the receiver, whereas existing polarimetric processing mainly
treats polarization as an additional estimation
dimension~\cite{nehorai1994vector,khan2020sixcomponent}. Second, acquisition
becomes most demanding in the large-aperture wideband regime that motivates
near-field processing: full-EVS training produces six field components over
$M_A$ sensors, $N$ subcarriers and $T$ RIS configurations, while an arbitrary
linear reduction provides no guarantee of preserving the delay subspace,
profiled likelihood or localization information. Fixed discretizations used by
sparse and codebook-based front ends~\cite{li2024vbi,cui2022channel} also
become increasingly coarse relative to the aperture resolution, while
restoring that resolution requires a denser dictionary and a larger search
burden. Third, the shared clock is either discarded or retained as an extra
nonlinear variable. Differencing cascaded OFDM delays removes the common
offset but leaves only $K-1$ differences for three position coordinates, which
is underdetermined at $K=3$, whereas retaining the absolute delays leaves a
nonconvex four-dimensional position--clock problem. Variable projection
eliminates the linear nuisances but not this coupling~\cite{golub1973varpro},
while the semidefinite route to a certified global solution~\cite{peng2025sdp}
addresses timing models that lift to a linear one, not the OFDM likelihood
here. The closest formulations reflect these limits: the nearest wideband
near-field RIS work estimates the channel without a
clock~\cite{yang2024nfris}, whereas the nearest near-field
localization-and-synchronization work considers one surface and a scalar
receive link~\cite{yan2025ris}. The architecture considered here exposes all
three limitations simultaneously.

We address these limitations through one structural property of the
cascaded observation rather than through three separate devices. The sensing block of
the $k$th RIS panel factorizes as
\begin{equation}
	\bA_k(\bchi)=
	\underbrace{\bc_k(\bchi)}_{\text{space--frequency geometry}}
	\otimes
	\underbrace{\bTheta_k^{({\rm mode})}}_{\text{calibrated Maxwell--Jones}},
	\label{eq:premise}
\end{equation}
where $\bchi=[\bp^{\mathsf T},\Delta t]^{\mathsf T}$ collects the unknown
position and common clock, $\bc_k$ carries all of that dependence, and
$\bTheta_k^{({\rm mode})}$ -- two columns for the polarization state, one row
per retained field component -- is fixed by the calibrated RIS-to-BS geometry
and the receiver selection. The unknown geometry and clock are therefore
separated from the calibrated polarimetric response. Three structural
consequences follow, and together they address the limitations above.
First, \emph{Maxwell--Jones information splitting}: the Kronecker form
splits the orthogonal projector into a geometric and a polarimetric term, so
receiver selection reweights a panel's free-Jones information only through
the polarization energy it retains and leaves the geometric kernel unchanged;
panel separability meanwhile obeys a separate subspace condition, and when
two or fewer field components are retained and the competing retained Jones
block has full row rank, panels sharing a space--frequency signature can lose
polarimetric separability entirely (C1, Theorem~\ref{thm:split}). Second,
\emph{Sufficient Maxwell--Kronecker compression}: the calibrated Maxwell
factors confine every admissible response to a parameter-independent receive
subspace of dimension at most twice the panel count, and projection onto that
subspace preserves the profiled likelihood maximizer, the parameter
derivatives of the residual criterion and the free-Jones information exactly
-- together with the noiseless delay poles, under the stated snapshot-rank
condition -- so acquisition proceeds on a representation that discards only
noise-only receive directions (C2, Theorem~\ref{thm:compress}). Third,
\emph{Certified common-clock profiling}: the shared clock acts as a common
unitary OFDM phase ramp that leaves the Jones Gram matrix invariant, reducing
the conditional clock dependence to a finite trigonometric polynomial whose
one-dimensional global maximization can be certified with a deterministic
optimality gap; the outer search then drops from four dimensions to three and
becomes invariant to the clock unit, though it remains local, the certificate
covering the conditional clock solve alone (C3, Theorem~\ref{thm:clock}).

Together these results give a two-phase estimator, though they do not enter
it in the same way. C2 is what the implementation runs on: it defines the
compression on which Phase~I builds a physically associated near-field
initializer, and it licenses Phase~II to evaluate the exact forward model on
that same compressed observation without approximation. C3 supplies
Phase~II's refinement, in which the Jones coefficients are eliminated
analytically by variable projection and the certified one-dimensional clock
profile removes the clock from the outer search, leaving a three-dimensional
position refinement regularized only in the Jones directions a reduced
receiver cannot see. C1 is not an algorithmic ingredient but a condition on
the receiver, stating which reduced EVS configurations retain enough
polarization structure for panel association to remain well posed.
Section~\ref{sec:simulation} tests that condition directly and, to
distinguish local precision from basin reliability, evaluates the
catastrophic tail alongside conditional precision.

Notation: bold symbols denote vectors/matrices; $(\cdot)^{\mathsf T}$,
$(\cdot)^{\mathsf H}$ and $(\cdot)^{\dagger}$ are transpose, Hermitian
transpose and pseudoinverse; $\otimes$ and $\odot$ are Kronecker and
Hadamard products; for a generic matrix $\bX$, $\bP_{\bX}=\bX\bX^{\dagger}$
and $\bPi_{\bX}^{\perp}=\bI-\bP_{\bX}$ are the orthogonal projectors onto
$\coll(\bX)$ and $\coll(\bX)^{\perp}$; and unqualified vector and matrix
norms are Euclidean and spectral, respectively.

\section{Maxwell--Jones Cascaded Signal Model}
\label{sec:system_model}

We consider a blocked line-of-sight (LoS) wideband uplink in which a
single-antenna UE reaches an $M_A$-element EVS uniform linear array (ULA) at
the BS only through $K$ distributed RIS panels of $M_R=M_{Rx}M_{Ry}$ elements
each~\cite{fascista2022rissync,zhao2021timing}. Each panel contributes one
dominant cascaded component, so the model order equals the known panel count
$K$. The BS and RIS panels are fixed, calibrated infrastructure, whereas the
UE position, a common UE--BS clock offset and the per-panel complex Jones
coefficients are unknown. The UE transmits pilots on $N$ subcarriers spaced
by $\Delta f$, giving an occupied bandwidth $B_{\rm occ}=(N-1)\Delta f$, over
$T$ known RIS phase configurations that all lie within one coherence block.
Fig.~\ref{fig:geometry} shows the reference geometry.

Each electrically large panel forms a one-sided near-field cascade: the
UE--RIS hop retains the exact spherical-wave phase, whereas the calibrated
RIS--BS hop is a center-frequency plane wave. For the reference geometry the
panel diagonal is $D\simeq0.226$~m and its Fraunhofer distance at
$f_c=60$~GHz is $R_{\rm F}=2D^2/\lambda_c=20.5$~m, so the $3.6$--$4.2$~m
UE--RIS links lie in the
radiative near field whereas the $28.6$--$31.2$~m RIS--BS links lie beyond
it~\cite{yang2024nfris,cui2022channel}.

\begin{figure}[!tb]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{EVS_RIS_system_model-crop.pdf}
\caption{Reference geometry and cascaded-path parameterization.
(a)~Three oriented $64\times64$ RIS panels relay the blocked-LoS UE signal to
a $16$-element EVS ULA. (b)~Local coordinates of the $k$th cascaded path
(Section~\ref{subsec:defchain}).}
\label{fig:geometry}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Calibrated Infrastructure Factor}
\label{subsec:calibrated}

Deployment calibration fixes every quantity entering the UE-independent
receive factor of panel $k$: the BS phase center $\bp_B$, the RIS panel
centers $\br_k$ and rotations $\bR_k\in{\rm SO}(3)$, the element coordinates
$\brho_{k,m}\in\R^3$ in the $k$th panel frame, and the BS array geometry and
EVS Maxwell response. Elements are centered on an $M_{Rx}\times M_{Ry}$ grid of
spacings $(d_x,d_y)$, the BS ULA lies along the global $x$ axis of the
orthonormal frame $\{\bm u_x,\bm u_y,\bm u_z\}$, and
$\kappa=2\pi/\lambda_c$ is the center-frequency wavenumber; explicit
coordinate and gauge conventions are in the supplement.

The calibrated locations give the RIS--BS distance and propagation direction
of panel $k$,
\begin{equation}
	d_{{\rm RB},k}=\|\bp_B-\br_k\|_2,
	\qquad
	\bs_{{\rm RB},k}=\frac{\bp_B-\br_k}{d_{{\rm RB},k}}
	\label{eq:rbgeom}
\end{equation}
and, under the far-field RIS--BS model established above, a plane-wave panel
response and a BS steering vector,
\begin{align}
	\big[\ba_{{\rm RB},k}\big]_m
	&=e^{-j\kappa\,\brho_{k,m}^{\mathsf T}\bR_k\bs_{{\rm RB},k}},\nonumber\\
	\big[\bv_{{\rm B},k}\big]_i
	&=e^{j\kappa x_i\,\bm u_x^{\mathsf T}\bs_{{\rm RB},k}},
	\label{eq:arb_vb}
\end{align}
with $x_i$ the $x$ coordinate of the $i$th BS element.

The same direction fixes the polarization manifold seen by the EVS array. A
transverse plane wave along $\bs_{{\rm RB},k}$ has Maxwell pair
$(\bE,\bH)=(\bq,\bs_{{\rm RB},k}\times\bq)$ in impedance-normalized units, so
with the transverse basis
\begin{equation}
	\bq_{k,1}=\frac{\bs_{{\rm RB},k}\times\bm u_z}
	{\|\bs_{{\rm RB},k}\times\bm u_z\|_2},
	\qquad
	\bq_{k,2}=\bs_{{\rm RB},k}\times\bq_{k,1},
	\label{eq:qbasis}
\end{equation}
and a fallback axis near the pole (supplement), the six-component
vector-sensor manifold~\cite{nehorai1994vector,khan2020sixcomponent}
specializes to the calibrated Maxwell--Jones block
\begin{equation}
	\bTheta_k^{(6)}=\begin{bmatrix}
	\bq_{k,1} & \bq_{k,2}\\[1pt]
	\bq_{k,2} & -\bq_{k,1}\end{bmatrix}\in\C^{6\times2},
	\label{eq:Theta}
\end{equation}
whose columns are orthogonal of squared norm two, so
$[\bTheta_k^{(6)}]^{\mathsf H}\bTheta_k^{(6)}=2\bI_2$, and any rotation of
the transverse basis is absorbed by the free Jones coefficient. A fixed
receiver selection $\bS_{\rm mode}\in\R^{d_{\rm evs}\times6}$ -- retaining
the first electric component ($d_{\rm evs}=1$, scalar), the first two
($d_{\rm evs}=2$, dual-polarized) or all six ($d_{\rm evs}=6$, full EVS) --
gives $\bTheta_k^{({\rm mode})}\triangleq\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_k^{(6)}
\in\C^{d_{\rm evs}\times2}$ and the receive dimension $I=d_{\rm evs}M_A$.

The calibrated receive block
$\bBm_k\triangleq\bv_{{\rm B},k}\otimes\bTheta_k^{({\rm mode})}\in\C^{I\times2}$
is therefore known before UE estimation and satisfies
$\partial\bBm_k/\partial\bchi=\mathbf0$; only the Jones-domain coefficients
$\bx_k$ remain unknown. All UE dependence enters through the near-field
wavefront and the OFDM delay developed next.

\subsection{UE Geometry and Common-Clock Factor}
\label{subsec:defchain}

The unknown nonlinear parameter collects the UE position and the single
clock, $\bchi=[\bp^{\mathsf T},\Delta t]^{\mathsf T}
=[x,y,z,\Delta t]^{\mathsf T}\in\R^4$, with $\bp$ in a known closed service
box $\mathcal P$ and $\Delta t$ in a known closed interval $\mathcal T$
shorter than the unambiguous delay period $1/\Delta f$. Every cascaded delay is a geometric
path length plus this one offset, shared by all panels as in asynchronous RIS
localization~\cite{fascista2022rissync,gong2024async,yan2025ris};
panel-dependent offsets~\cite{zhao2021timing} lie outside the model.
Seen in the $k$th panel frame the UE is at $\bxi_k(\bp)=\bR_k(\bp-\br_k)$,
from which
\begin{equation}
	d_{{\rm UR},k}(\bp)=\|\bxi_k(\bp)\|_2,
	\qquad
	\bs_{{\rm UR},k}(\bp)=\frac{\bxi_k(\bp)}{d_{{\rm UR},k}(\bp)}
	\label{eq:urgeom}
\end{equation}
with $d_{{\rm UR},k}=\|\bp-\br_k\|_2$ because $\bR_k$ is a rotation. Panel
phase shifts are scalar and identical for the two polarizations, so a panel
redirects a wave without mixing its Jones components, an RIS model rather
than a general property; with prescribed
constant-modulus training
$[\bOmega_k]_{t,m}=e^{j\omega_{k,t,m}}/\sqrt{M_R}$,
$\bOmega_k\in\C^{T\times M_R}$~\cite{lin2021tensor}, the near-field
dependence is captured by the RIS response
\begin{equation}
	\bh_k(\bp) = \bOmega_k\,\bg_k(\bp),
	\qquad
	\bg_k(\bp) = \ba_{{\rm RB},k}\odot\ba^{\rm NF}_{{\rm UR},k}(\bp),
	\label{eq:hk}
\end{equation}
where
\begin{align}
	\big[\ba^{\rm NF}_{{\rm UR},k}(\bp)\big]_m
	&= e^{-j\frac{2\pi}{\lambda_c}\delta_{k,m}(\bp)},\nonumber\\
	\delta_{k,m}(\bp)
	&= \big\|\bxi_k(\bp)-\brho_{k,m}\big\|_2
	- d_{{\rm UR},k}(\bp),
	\label{eq:delta}
\end{align}
is the exact wavefront difference between element $m$ and the panel center.
Equation~\eqref{eq:delta} retains the exact spherical-wave phase across the
panel aperture; the Fresnel approximation enters only in Phase-I proposal
generation (Section~\ref{subsec:beamspace}).

Combining \eqref{eq:rbgeom} and \eqref{eq:urgeom}, the cascaded delay and
its OFDM phase ramp are
\begin{align}
	\tau_k(\bp,\Delta t)
	&= \underbrace{\frac{d_{{\rm UR},k}(\bp) + d_{{\rm RB},k}}{c_0}}_{
	\textstyle \tau_{g,k}(\bp)} + \Delta t,\nonumber\\
	\bd_k(\bp,\Delta t)
	&= \big[\,e^{-j2\pi n\Delta f\,\tau_k(\bp,\Delta t)}\,\big]_{n=0}^{N-1}
	\in\C^N,
	\label{eq:dk}
\end{align}
with $c_0$ the speed of light. The per-subcarrier form of \eqref{eq:dk}
assumes the cyclic prefix covers the cascaded delay spread and neglects
residual carrier-frequency offset and phase noise. Spatial manifolds are
evaluated at the carrier $f_c$ while this delay phase is retained on every
subcarrier. Once the common center-frequency phase is absorbed into the path
gain the tones are symmetric about $f_c$, so with $\Delta L$ an inter-element
path difference of a spatial response the neglected spatial-wideband phase is
\begin{equation}
	\epsilon_{\rm sq}=\frac{\pi B_{\rm occ}}{c_0}\max|\Delta L|,
	\label{eq:squint}
\end{equation}
which peaks at $0.102$~rad over the three spatial responses of the reference
configuration (design condition and per-panel audit in the supplement).

The Kronecker product of the RIS training/near-field response, the OFDM
delay ramp and the calibrated receive block is the
premise~\eqref{eq:premise}:
\begin{equation}
	\bA_k(\bchi)
	= \bh_k(\bp)\otimes\bd_k(\bp,\Delta t)\otimes\bBm_k
	= \bc_k(\bchi)\otimes\bTheta_k^{({\rm mode})},
	\label{eq:Ak}
\end{equation}
where, by associativity of the Kronecker product,
$\bc_k(\bchi)=\bh_k(\bp)\otimes\bd_k(\bp,\Delta t)\otimes
\bv_{{\rm B},k}\in\C^{TNM_A}$ is the space--frequency factor. The scalar
sample expansion and the stacking convention that reproduces \eqref{eq:Ak},
including pilot normalization, are in the supplement.

Equation~\eqref{eq:Ak} admits two useful groupings. The first,
$\bh_k\otimes\bd_k\otimes\bBm_k$, exposes the calibrated receive subspace:
since
$\bA_k\bx_k=\bh_k\otimes\bd_k\otimes(\bBm_k\bx_k)$, reading the $I$ receive
channels against the $N$ subcarriers at configuration $t$ arranges the same
samples as
\begin{equation}
	\bY_t=\sum_{k=1}^{K}[\bh_k(\bp)]_t\,(\bBm_k\bx_k)\,
	\bd_k(\bp,\Delta t)^{\mathsf T}+\bN_t,
	\label{eq:obsmat}
\end{equation}
$t=1,\ldots,T$, with $\bY_t\in\C^{I\times N}$ and $\bN_t$ the noise block. Each panel is therefore rank
one across receive and frequency at fixed $t$, its receive factor confined to
the $\bchi$-independent $\coll(\bBm_k)$ of dimension at most two; this is the
arrangement Section~\ref{sec:algorithm} works in. The second grouping,
$\bc_k\otimes\bTheta_k^{({\rm mode})}$, confines all dependence on
$\bS_{\rm mode}$ to $\bTheta_k^{({\rm mode})}$ and all position--clock
dependence to the mode-independent $\bc_k(\bchi)$, with $\Delta t$ entering
only through $\bd_k$; this is the arrangement Section~\ref{sec:analysis}
works in.

\subsection{Structured Observation Model and Estimation Problem}
\label{subsec:obsmodel}

Stacking the panels as
$\bA(\bchi)=[\bA_1(\bchi),\ldots,\bA_K(\bchi)]\in\C^{M\times 2K}$ and
vectorizing the $\bY_t$ in the same order gives
\begin{equation}
	\by = \bmu(\bchi,\bx) + \bn = \bA(\bchi)\,\bx + \bn,
	\qquad
	\bn\sim\mathcal{CN}(\mathbf 0,\sigma^2\bI_M),
	\label{eq:obs}
\end{equation}
where $M=I\,N\,T$ and the noise is white across receive channels,
subcarriers and RIS configurations, with power $\sigma^2$ \emph{not}
provided to the estimator. The unknown Jones-domain coefficient of path $k$
is $\bx_k=\beta_k\be_k\in\C^2$, stacked as
$\bx=[\bx_1^{\mathsf T},\ldots,\bx_K^{\mathsf T}]^{\mathsf T}\in\C^{2K}$,
where the complex gain $\beta_k$ absorbs path loss, RIS efficiency and the
center-frequency phase of the cascade and $\be_k$ is a unit Jones state
(angular parameterization in the supplement). Both factors are flat over the
occupied band and only their product is identifiable, since $(\beta_k,\be_k)$
and $(\beta_ke^{j\phi},e^{-j\phi}\be_k)$ give the same $\bx_k$, so the
estimator treats $\bx_k$ as a free $\C^2$ nuisance.

Profiling the unknown $\sigma^2$ out of the white-Gaussian log-likelihood
gives
\begin{equation}
	\hat\sigma^2(\bchi,\bx)=\tfrac{1}{M}\|\by-\bA(\bchi)\bx\|_2^2,
	\quad
	\ln p\big|_{\hat\sigma^2}=-M\big[\ln(\pi\hat\sigma^2)+1\big],
	\label{eq:sigmaprofile}
\end{equation}
strictly decreasing in the residual energy, so maximum-likelihood
(ML) estimation of $(\bchi,\bx)$ is the least-squares problem
\begin{equation}
	\min_{\bp\in\mathcal P,\ \Delta t\in\mathcal T,\ \bx\in\C^{2K}}
	\big\|\by-\bA(\bp,\Delta t)\,\bx\big\|_2^2.
	\label{eq:ml_problem}
\end{equation}
The $2K$ Jones coefficients enter linearly and admit closed-form
elimination, whereas position and clock enter nonlinearly through
\eqref{eq:delta} and \eqref{eq:dk}, leaving \eqref{eq:ml_problem} nonconvex
in four real coordinates.

\section{Proposed Estimator: Sufficient Acquisition and Certified Clock Profiling}
\label{sec:algorithm}

Direct optimization of \eqref{eq:ml_problem} mixes four distinct roles in
one nonconvex problem: the physical panel permutation, the near-field
basin, the $2K$ linear Jones coefficients and the common clock. We
separate them structurally. Two elements of the acquisition literature are
retained -- an algebraic front end that recovers the delay poles from a
frequency-domain shift invariance, and a coarse-to-fine spherical search -- while the unstructured
receive reduction and the fixed grid that usually accompany them are
replaced. Phase~I recovers the delay poles and their coupled factors inside
a sufficient receive subspace, associates them with physical panels, and
enforces one common UE geometry; Phase~II eliminates the Jones
coefficients analytically and profiles the clock along its exact unitary
orbit. We call the two phases
\emph{Maxwell--Kronecker sufficient compression with common-geometry
initialization} (MKSC-GI) and \emph{common-clock orbit-profiled Jones
variable projection} (CCOP-JVP).

\subsection{Maxwell--Kronecker Factor Recovery}

\textit{1) Sufficient compression and delay-pole recovery:}
Position and clock enter each cascaded delay only through their sum, so no
single cascaded delay separates them and Phase~I begins from the delay poles.
Two structural facts of \eqref{eq:Ak} make those poles recoverable from
\eqref{eq:obsmat} with no knowledge of $\bchi$.

First, the receive dimension reduces without loss. Every column of $\bY_t$
is a combination of the calibrated vectors $\bBm_k\bx_k$, so all admissible
responses lie in $\mathcal S_R=\sum_{k}\coll(\bBm_k)$, of dimension
$r\le2K$, and the orthogonal receive directions carry noise but no signal.
Let $\bQ_S\in\C^{I\times r}$ be an orthonormal basis of $\mathcal S_R$
obtained from $\bPsi=[\bBm_1,\ldots,\bBm_K]$, hence fixed by calibration
alone. Compressing the receive index alone gives
\begin{equation}
	\bY_{{\rm d},t}=\bQ_S^{\mathsf H}\bY_t
	=\sum_{k=1}^{K}[\bh_k(\bp)]_t\,\ba_k\,
	\bd_k(\bp,\Delta t)^{\mathsf T}+\bQ_S^{\mathsf H}\bN_t,
	\label{eq:obsmat_c}
\end{equation}
with $\ba_k=\bQ_S^{\mathsf H}\bBm_k\bx_k\in\C^{r}$: this is
\eqref{eq:obsmat} in receive dimension $r$ rather than $I$, with
$\bQ_S^{\mathsf H}\bN_t$ still white and nothing lost by
Theorem~\ref{thm:compress}.

Second, the delay of path $k$ reaches the observation only through the
single pole $z_k=e^{-j2\pi\Delta f\tau_k}$, so its OFDM ramp
$\bd_k=[1,z_k,\ldots,z_k^{N-1}]^{\mathsf T}$ is Vandermonde. Reading the
$rT$ compressed channel--configuration pairs of the $\bY_{{\rm d},t}$ as
snapshots against the $N$ tones gives the frequency matrix
\begin{equation}
	\bY_f=\sum_{k=1}^{K}\bd_k\,\bw_k^{\mathsf T}+\bN_f\in\C^{N\times rT},
	\qquad
	\bw_k=\ba_k\otimes\bh_k(\bp),
	\label{eq:freqsnap}
\end{equation}
in which every dependence on $\bchi$ outside $\bd_k$ is confined to the
snapshot vector $\bw_k$. Writing $\bD=[\bd_1,\ldots,\bd_K]$ and
$\underline{\bD}$, $\overline{\bD}$ for $\bD$ without its last and first
row, the $K$ poles obey an exact shift invariance on the tone index,
\begin{equation}
	\overline{\bD}=\underline{\bD}\,\boldsymbol\Phi,
	\qquad
	\boldsymbol\Phi=\diag(z_1,\ldots,z_K),
	\label{eq:shift}
\end{equation}
which subspace methods read off directly~\cite{roy1989esprit,gong2026aimdf}.
Under cascaded delays distinct modulo $1/\Delta f$, at least $K+1$ tones and
the snapshot-rank condition of Theorem~\ref{thm:compress}(iv) applied to
$[\bw_1,\ldots,\bw_K]$, the $K$ delays are thus identifiable from the
frequency axis alone.

\textit{2) Coupled factor recovery:}
Forward--backward total-least-squares estimation of signal parameters via
rotational invariance techniques
(ESPRIT)~\cite{roy1989esprit,gong2026aimdf} then recovers
$\boldsymbol\Phi$ of \eqref{eq:shift} from $\bY_f$, each estimated pole
projected onto the unit circle. Once the $\hat z_k$ are fixed, $\bD$ is known and the coupled column
$\bw_k=\ba_k\otimes\bh_k$ of \eqref{eq:freqsnap} enters linearly, so the
coupled columns are recovered jointly by one weighted least-squares solve
rather than by alternating factor updates. Writing $\bY_{rT,N}\in\C^{rT\times N}$ for the
rearrangement of the $\bY_{{\rm d},t}$ that groups the compressed receive
and training indices as rows against the tone index, the coupled factors
follow from
\begin{equation}
 \hat\bF=\bY_{rT,N}\bW\bD^{\ast}
 (\bD^{\mathsf T}\bW\bD^{\ast}+\lambda_\tau\bI)^{-1},
 \label{eq:coupledls_short}
\end{equation}
with loading $\lambda_\tau$ fixed in the supplement and
$\bW=\diag(w_0,\ldots,w_{N-1})$ the frequency-smoothing weights, $w_n$
the anti-diagonal multiplicity $\#\{(p,\ell):p+\ell=n\}$ of a $P\times L$
window with $P+L-1=N$. Smoothing therefore enters as a tone weighting --
\eqref{eq:coupledls_short} is identically the solve on the smoothed array
the implementation stores -- and no canonical polyadic or other global
multilinear decomposition of the training tensor is formed. Separating the
two factors inside a recovered column is a local operation instead. With
$\unvec_{r\times T}(\cdot)$ the reshaping of a column into $\C^{r\times T}$
and $\unvec_{r\times T}(\hat\bF_{:,j})
=\sum_i\sigma_{i,j}\bu_{i,j}\bv_{i,j}^{\mathsf H}$ its singular value
decomposition,
\begin{equation}
	\tilde\ba_j=\sqrt{\sigma_{1,j}}\,\bQ_S\bu_{1,j},
	\quad
	\tilde\bt_j=\sqrt{\sigma_{1,j}}\,\bv_{1,j}^{\ast},
	\quad
	\varrho_j=\frac{\sigma_{2,j}}{\sigma_{1,j}},
	\label{eq:split_rank1}
\end{equation}
with $\tilde\ba_j\in\C^{I}$ the EVS space--polarization factor lifted back by
$\bQ_S$, $\tilde\bt_j\in\C^{T}$ the RIS training factor, and the leading
singular value split symmetrically between them. The rank-one defect $\varrho_j$ is the per-column confidence that
weights the common-geometry fit below.

\textit{3) Physical panel association:}
The recovered columns are algebraically unlabeled: nothing ties column $j$
to panel $k$, yet every downstream step needs that tie. Panel association is therefore
performed before any geometry is fused, by searching permutations under a
composite cost that combines three complementary cues: Maxwell-subspace
consistency identifies the panel-dependent receive manifold, spherical-wave
consistency tests the panel-local geometry, and the common-clock replicas
enforce a joint cross-panel constraint. For column $j$ and panel $k$,
\begin{equation}
 s_{j,k}=
 \frac{\|\bPi^{\perp}_{\bBm_k}\tilde\ba_j\|}{\|\tilde\ba_j\|}
 +\min_{\boldsymbol\eta\in\mathcal E_k}
 \frac{\|\bPi^{\perp}_{\bh_k(\boldsymbol\eta)}\tilde\bt_j\|}
 {\|\tilde\bt_j\|},
 \label{eq:assoc_score}
\end{equation}
with $\bBm_k$ the calibrated receive block of panel $k$ defined in
Section~\ref{subsec:calibrated}, $\bh_k(\boldsymbol\eta)$ the exact
near-field panel response \eqref{eq:hk} in the panel-local coordinates
$\boldsymbol\eta_k=(d_{{\rm UR},k},u_k,v_k)$ -- range and the first two
components of $\bs_{{\rm UR},k}$, in bijection with $\bxi_k$ in front of the
panel -- and $\mathcal E_k$ the local search box over them; both terms are relative residual norms in $[0,1]$, so they combine
without a calibration constant. Writing $\hat d_{j,k}$ for the range coordinate of the minimizer
in \eqref{eq:assoc_score} and $\hat\tau_j$ for the delay of the estimated
pole, each pair also implies a clock replica
$\widehat{\Delta t}_{j,k}=\hat\tau_j-(\hat d_{j,k}+d_{{\rm RB},k})/c_0$,
and a permutation is scored by
\begin{equation}
 C(\pi)=\sum_{j=1}^{K}s_{j,\pi(j)}
 +\frac{w_{\rm clk}}{t_0}
 \big[\,\mathrm{sd}(\pi)+e_{\mathcal T}(\pi)\big],
 \label{eq:assoc_perm}
\end{equation}
where $\mathrm{sd}(\pi)$ is the spread of the $K$ replicas
$\widehat{\Delta t}_{j,\pi(j)}$ about their mean, $e_{\mathcal T}(\pi)$ is
their mean excursion outside $\mathcal T$, and $w_{\rm clk}=1/2$,
$t_0=1$~ns. Because the clock term couples all $K$ assignments at once,
$C(\pi)$ does not reduce to a linear assignment, so the labels are returned
by a deterministic two-tier search rather than by a global minimization of
$C$ under the exact criterion. Let $C^{\rm cb}$ be \eqref{eq:assoc_perm} with
the near-field term of \eqref{eq:assoc_score} evaluated on the cached coarse
codebook, and let $\pi_{(1)},\pi_{(2)}$ be its two best permutations among
all $K!$. The second tier re-scores only the column--panel pairs those two
contain, with the exact spherical projection, and returns
\begin{equation}
	\hat\pi=\arg\min_{\pi\in\{\pi_{(1)},\pi_{(2)}\}}C(\pi),
	\qquad
	m_{\rm assoc}=\big|C(\pi_{(1)})-C(\pi_{(2)})\big|
	\label{eq:assoc_two_tier}
\end{equation}
with $m_{\rm assoc}$ the reported margin (supplement). The first tier is the one part of the estimator whose
cost grows factorially in $K$, negligible at $K=3$ but a real limit on panel
count. The front end therefore outputs $K$ delay--factor triples
$\{\hat\tau_j,\tilde\ba_j,\tilde\bt_j\}$ carrying physical panel labels
$\hat\pi$, the input to the geometric acquisition below.

\subsection{Beam-Space Acquisition and Common-Geometry Initialization}
\label{subsec:beamspace}

\textit{1) Panel-local beam-space acquisition:}
Labeled factors are still only training-domain vectors; the geometry they
encode must be read back out through the model of
Section~\ref{subsec:defchain}, one panel at a time, before the $K$ readings
can be reconciled into a single position. With $\tilde\bt_k$ the
training-domain factor that $\hat\pi$ assigns to panel $k$, its local
range--direction estimate minimizes
\begin{equation}
 J_{\rm RIS}(\boldsymbol\eta)
 =\|\bPi_{\bh_k(\boldsymbol\eta)}^\perp\tilde\bt_k\|^2,
 \quad \bh_k(\boldsymbol\eta)=\bOmega_k
 [\ba_{{\rm RB},k}\odot\ba^{\rm NF}(\boldsymbol\eta)],
 \label{eq:Jris_short}
\end{equation}
over the panel-local coordinates $\boldsymbol\eta$ defined above. Because a
fixed-count angular dictionary of the polar or beam-training
kind~\cite{cui2022channel,zheng2025wideband} has a step set by the search box
rather than by the panel resolution $\lambda_c/D$, the dominant angular
search is moved instead to the element domain. Because the training code
enters $\bh_k$ only on
the left, the numerator correlation of \eqref{eq:Jris_short} satisfies the
identity
\begin{equation}
 \bh_k(\boldsymbol\eta)^{\mathsf H}\tilde\bt_k
 =\big[\ba^{\rm NF}(\boldsymbol\eta)\big]^{\mathsf H}\tilde\bg_k,
 \quad
 \tilde\bg_k=\ba_{{\rm RB},k}^{\ast}\odot
 (\bOmega_k^{\mathsf H}\tilde\bt_k),
 \label{eq:adjoint_short}
\end{equation}
so one adjoint pass moves the factor into the element domain for every
subsequent trial geometry, an exact rewriting of the correlation and not an
inversion of $\bOmega_k$. Expanding \eqref{eq:delta} to second order about
the panel center separates the residual phase into a part linear in the
direction cosines $\bu$ and a curvature part set by the trial range $\tilde d$,
\begin{equation}
	\delta_{k,m}\simeq-\brho_{k,m}^{\mathsf T}\bu
	+\frac{\|\brho_{k,m}\|^2-(\brho_{k,m}^{\mathsf T}\bu)^2}{2\tilde d},
	\label{eq:fresnel}
\end{equation}
so the paraxial dechirp
$\tilde\bg_{k,\tilde d}[m]=\tilde\bg_k[m]e^{j\kappa\|\brho_{k,m}\|^2/(2\tilde d)}$
removes the isotropic quadratic term and leaves the plane-wave phase
$e^{j\kappa\brho_{k,m}^{\mathsf T}\bu}$ as the dominant angular dependence,
together with a residual direction-dependent curvature in
$(\brho_{k,m}^{\mathsf T}\bu)^2/(2\tilde d)$. One zero-padded two-dimensional
discrete Fourier transform (2-D DFT) then scores every direction cosine at
once, on a grid that tracks the Rayleigh limit at a cost decoupled from any
dictionary size. Because of that residual, and because range resolves more
weakly than direction, \eqref{eq:fresnel} only proposes: each visible angular
peak receives an exact spherical range scan and is re-scored by
\eqref{eq:Jris_short} before local refinement, so the approximation never
replaces the spherical model.

The default MKSC-GI candidate set contains the beam-space proposals only;
for the small-aperture sweeps of Section~\ref{subsec:res_setup} an
explicitly reported variant unions them with the fixed-count dictionary.
All candidates are evaluated by the same exact criterion
\eqref{eq:Jris_short}, so the candidate set changes acquisition coverage and
cost, not the forward model.

\textit{2) Common-geometry fusion:}
Each panel-local fix $\hat{\boldsymbol\eta}_k$ maps through the calibrated
panel pose to a global position $\hat\bp_k^{\rm loc}$. These $K$ positions
generally disagree; the common-geometry stage replaces them by the single
position that best explains all $K$ training factors at once.
Unknown complex factor scales are eliminated in the objective
\begin{align}
 J_{\rm geom}(\bp)
 &=\sum_{k=1}^K \varpi_k\left[\|\tilde\bt_k\|^2-
 \frac{|\bh_k^{\mathsf H}(\bp)\tilde\bt_k|^2}{\|\bh_k(\bp)\|^2}\right]
 \label{eq:Jgeom_short}\\
 	 &=\min_{\balpha\in\C^K}\sum_{k=1}^K \varpi_k
 \|\tilde\bt_k-\alpha_k\bh_k(\bp)\|^2,\nonumber
\end{align}
whose profiled scale
$\hat\alpha_k=\bh_k^{\mathsf H}\tilde\bt_k/\|\bh_k\|^2$ makes the two lines
identical, so $J_{\rm geom}$ sees only the component of $\tilde\bt_k$
orthogonal to $\coll(\bh_k)$ and is blind to the unknown path gains. The
weights $\varpi_k=\max\{e^{-\varrho_k/\varrho_0},\varpi_{\min}\}$ down-weight a column
whose rank-one split is poor while keeping every panel in the fit. We minimize
\eqref{eq:Jgeom_short} with bounded L-BFGS-B~\cite{byrd1995lbfgsb} from a
small deterministic set of starts built from the clipped coordinate-wise
median
$\hat\bp_0=\clip_{\mathcal P}\{\median_k\hat\bp_k^{\rm loc}\}$; the
constants are supplementary.

\textit{3) Jones-anchor refresh:}
At the selected $\hat\bp_{\rm GI}$, the $\lambda_k=0$ instance of the
certified clock profiler of Section~\ref{subsec:ccop} refreshes the Jones
directions at a fixed position,
\begin{equation}
 (\hat{\Delta t}_{\rm ref},\hat\bx_{\rm ref})=
 \arg\min_{\Delta t\in\mathcal T,\bx}
 \|\by-\bA(\hat\bp_{\rm GI},\Delta t)\bx\|^2.
 \label{eq:refresh_short}
\end{equation}
With $\hat\bx_{{\rm ref},k}$ the $k$th two-entry block of $\hat\bx_{\rm ref}$,
the anchor is $\hat\be_k=\hat\bx_{{\rm ref},k}/\|\hat\bx_{{\rm ref},k}\|_2$,
obtained at the common position from a conditional clock solve that carries
the same optimality-gap certificate. Phase~I therefore outputs
$\hat\bp_{\rm GI}$ and the anchors $\{\hat\be_k\}$, which initialize the
Phase-II directional regularizer.

\subsection{Common-Clock Orbit-Profiled Jones Variable Projection}
\label{subsec:ccop}

\textit{1) Unitary clock orbit and directional Jones regularization:}
Phase~II returns from the basin just built to the exact forward model
underlying \eqref{eq:ml_problem}, augmented by a directional Jones penalty.
Its $2K$ Jones coefficients enter linearly and are eliminated in
closed form by variable projection~\cite{golub1973varpro}, leaving four
nonlinear coordinates. By Theorem~\ref{thm:compress} the criterion may be
evaluated on the compressed observation of Phase~I without approximation,
which is what the implementation does. The common clock is the only nonlinear
coordinate along which the likelihood admits an exact unitary symmetry. Let
$\bA_0(\bp)\triangleq\bA(\bp,0)$ and
$\bD_{\rm clk}(\Delta t)=\diag([e^{-j2\pi n\Delta f\Delta t}]_{n=0}^{N-1})$.
Then
\begin{equation}
 \bU_{\rm clk}(\Delta t)=\bI_T\otimes\bD_{\rm clk}(\Delta t)\otimes\bI_I,
 \quad \bA(\bp,\Delta t)=\bU_{\rm clk}(\Delta t)\bA_0(\bp),
 \label{eq:orbit_short}
\end{equation}
and unitarity makes the Jones Gram clock invariant,
$\bA^{\mathsf H}\bA=\bA_0^{\mathsf H}\bA_0\triangleq\bG_0(\bp)$.
Phase~II uses a Gram-scaled block-diagonal penalty
\begin{equation}
 \bR_{\rm dir}(\bp)=\blkdiag_k\left[
 \lambda_k\tfrac12\tr\bG_{0,kk}(\bp)\,\bPi_{\hat\be_k}^{\perp}\right],
 \label{eq:Rdir_short}
\end{equation}
which penalizes only the component of $\bx_k$ orthogonal to the refreshed
anchor $\hat\be_k$. Phase~I supplies a Jones-direction anchor while the path
gain stays a free nuisance, so a projector preserves the former without
constraining the latter, which a penalty on
$\|\bx_k-\hat\bx_{{\rm ref},k}\|$ would not. Its function is to keep the
inner problem well posed as the receiver is reduced. Full-EVS reception gives
$[\bTheta_k^{(6)}]^{\mathsf H}\bTheta_k^{(6)}=2\bI_2$ by \eqref{eq:Theta},
so every $\bG_{0,kk}$ is a scaled identity and the penalty is inert; a mask
with $d_{\rm evs}<2$ leaves $\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_k^{(6)}$ of rank one, so
$\bG_{0,kk}$ is singular and one Jones coordinate per panel is invisible to
the data. Supported on the complement of $\hat\be_k$, $\bR_{\rm dir}$ fixes
that coordinate at the Phase-I anchor without displacing the identifiable
one: it selects one representative among minimizers the data cannot
separate, which is how it secures the $\bG_\lambda\succ\mathbf0$ hypothesis of
Theorem~\ref{thm:clock} while leaving the attained residual unchanged. The
dimensionless weight is $\lambda_k=1$ throughout, so the penalty enters at
the scale of the per-path data Gram (Remark~\ref{rem:peb_reading}).

\textit{2) Jones variable projection and the trigonometric clock profile:}
Phase~II accordingly minimizes the penalized exact-forward-model criterion
\begin{equation}
 J(\bp,\Delta t,\bx)=\|\by-\bA(\bp,\Delta t)\bx\|_2^2
 +\bx^{\mathsf H}\bR_{\rm dir}(\bp)\bx,
 \label{eq:penalized_short}
\end{equation}
whose first term is the nominal criterion \eqref{eq:ml_problem}. It is
\eqref{eq:penalized_short}, not \eqref{eq:ml_problem}, that
Theorem~\ref{thm:clock} nests and certifies; the two coincide at
$\lambda_k=0$, and Section~\ref{subsec:res_c1} reports the $\lambda$ sweep.
With
$\bG_\lambda=\bG_0+\bR_{\rm dir}$, which reduces to $\bG_0$ at
$\lambda_k=0$, and
$\bb=\bA_0^{\mathsf H}\bU_{\rm clk}^{\mathsf H}\by$, the inner minimizer is
$\hat\bx=\bG_\lambda^{-1}\bb$ and analytic Jones elimination gives
$F(\bp,\Delta t)\triangleq\min_{\bx\in\C^{2K}}J=\|\by\|^2-Q(\bp,\Delta t)$
with $Q=\bb^{\mathsf H}\bG_\lambda^{-1}\bb$, hence the 3-D outer objective
\begin{equation}
 \widetilde F(\bp)=\|\by\|^2-
 \max_{\Delta t\in\mathcal T}Q(\bp,\Delta t).
 \label{eq:nested_short}
\end{equation}
Grouping the sufficient-statistic correlations by tone as
$\bb(\bp,\Delta t)=\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}\bu_n(\bp)
e^{j2\pi n\Delta f\Delta t}$, the quadratic form through the
clock-independent $\bG_\lambda^{-1}$ retains only tone differences $\ell=n'-n$.
Hence, for fixed $\bp$, $Q$ is a real trigonometric polynomial of degree at
most $N-1$,
\begin{equation}
 Q(\bp,\Delta t)=\sum_{\ell=-(N-1)}^{N-1}\gamma_\ell(\bp)
 e^{j2\pi\ell\Delta f\Delta t},\quad \gamma_{-\ell}=\gamma_\ell^\ast,
 \label{eq:trig_short}
\end{equation}
so one factorization of $\bG_\lambda$ and one zero-padded FFT supply all initial
clock candidates. With
$L_2=(2\pi\Delta f)^2\sum_\ell\ell^2|\gamma_\ell|$, an interval centered at
$t_c$ with half-width $r_t$ obeys
\begin{equation}
 Q(t)\le Q(t_c)+|Q'(t_c)|r_t+\tfrac12L_2r_t^2.
 \label{eq:bound_short}
\end{equation}
\textit{3) Certified clock maximization and outer refinement:}
Branch-and-bound maintains \eqref{eq:bound_short} over $\mathcal T$ and
returns an incumbent with
$Q(\Delta t^\star)-Q(\hat{\Delta t})\le U-Q(\hat{\Delta t})
\triangleq g_{\rm cert}$, where $U$ is the largest surviving interval upper
bound; subdivision stops once $g_{\rm cert}\le\epsilon_{\rm clk}$ or a cap
on subdivisions is reached. Every outer position therefore carries a
deterministic gap certificate, $\epsilon_{\rm clk}$-optimal whenever
termination is by tolerance, as Theorem~\ref{thm:clock}(iii) states. The outer bounded search starts from $\hat\bp_{\rm GI}$ and, where the
maximizing branch is unique and interior, uses the envelope-theorem gradient
\begin{equation}
	\nabla_{\bp}\widetilde F(\bp)
	=-\nabla_{\bp}Q(\bp,\Delta t)\big|_{\Delta t=\hat{\Delta t}(\bp)},
	\label{eq:envelope}
\end{equation}
with a derivative-free safeguard~\cite{powell1964direct} otherwise. Phase~II outputs
$(\hat\bp,\widehat{\Delta t},\hat\bx)$ together with the certificate attached
to every conditional clock solve it evaluated. Tolerances, the safeguard
trigger and pseudocode for both phases are supplementary.


\section{Structural Analysis and Guarantees}
\label{sec:analysis}

This section formalizes C1--C3 under the conditions stated with each result.
We first define the free-Jones reference information and its local rank
condition; the three guarantees then follow in turn from the Kronecker
projector structure of \eqref{eq:premise}, from its calibrated receive
subspace, and from its common-clock unitary orbit. Computational scaling is
summarized last.

\textit{A. Reference information and identifiability:}
Let $\bD_\chi=[\partial(\bA\bx)/\partial\chi_i]_{i=1}^4$. Eliminating the
unknown complex Jones coefficients from the white-Gaussian Fisher
information matrix gives the free-Jones equivalent Fisher information
matrix (EFIM), the reference information of the unpenalized data model,
\begin{equation}
 \bJ_\chi^{\rm free}=\frac{2}{\sigma^2}
 \Real\{\bD_\chi^{\mathsf H}\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi\}.
 \label{eq:efim_short}
\end{equation}
Local position--clock identifiability after eliminating the Jones nuisance
requires
\begin{equation}
 \rank_{\R}[\Real\{\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi\}^{\mathsf T},
 \Imag\{\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi\}^{\mathsf T}]^{\mathsf T}=4,
 \label{eq:rankcond}
\end{equation}
which is exactly $\bJ_\chi^{\rm free}\succ\mathbf0$, since
$\Real\{\bD_\chi^{\mathsf H}\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi\}$ is the Gram matrix
of that real stacking. When $\bJ_\chi^{\rm free}\succ\mathbf0$, partitioning
its position and clock blocks,
$\bJ_p^{\rm eff}=\bJ_{pp}-\bJ_{pt}\bJ_{tt}^{-1}\bJ_{tp}$, gives the position
and clock error bounds (PEB, CEB),
${\rm PEB}=\sqrt{\tr[(\bJ_p^{\rm eff})^{-1}]}$ and
${\rm CEB}=\sqrt{[(\bJ_\chi^{\rm free})^{-1}]_{4,4}}$. The delay-only
Jacobian of \eqref{eq:dk} is
\begin{equation}
	\bD_\tau(\bp)
	=\frac{\partial[\tau_1,\ldots,\tau_K]^{\mathsf T}}
	{\partial\bchi^{\mathsf T}}
	=\begin{bmatrix}
	c_0^{-1}\bs_{{\rm UR},1}^{\mathsf T}\bR_1 & 1\\
	\vdots & \vdots\\
	c_0^{-1}\bs_{{\rm UR},K}^{\mathsf T}\bR_K & 1
	\end{bmatrix}\in\R^{K\times4},
	\label{eq:delayjac}
\end{equation}
whose all-ones clock column is exactly what row differencing removes. At
$K=3$, $\rank\bD_\tau\le3$, so $\dim\ker\bD_\tau\ge1$ and the cascaded
delays cannot identify $\bchi$ by themselves; the near-field aperture
supplies the unresolved direction, since after complex-scale elimination the
projected tangents
$\bPi_{\bh_k}^{\perp}
[\partial\bh_k/\partial x,\partial\bh_k/\partial y,
\partial\bh_k/\partial z]$ retain the phase slope and curvature of
\eqref{eq:delta}. The scope is local and free-Jones: the pseudoinverse
projectors cover the rank-deficient Jones blocks that a scalar or dual mask
produces, gains and Jones directions are unknown deterministic nuisances,
and global panel-permutation and finite-bandwidth ambiguities lie outside
this rank test, which decides in any given geometry whether the missing
direction is in fact supplied. A derivation of \eqref{eq:efim_short} and of
the real-rank equivalence is supplementary.

\textit{B. C1---Maxwell--Jones information splitting:}
Both parts of C1 rest on one identity. Because $\bA_k$ is a Kronecker
product its Gram factors, $\bA_k^{\mathsf H}\bA_k=(\bc_k^{\mathsf H}\bc_k)
[\bTheta_k^{\mathsf H}\bTheta_k]$, and so does its projector; the complement
then separates into a polarization-orthogonal component along the geometric
factor and a geometry-orthogonal component that acts identically on every
polarization.

\begin{lemma}[Kronecker projector splitting]
\label{lem:kron}
Let $\bc\neq\mathbf0$ and $\bTheta\neq\mathbf0$ be arbitrary, with
$\bP_{\bTheta}=\bTheta\bTheta^{\dagger}$. With $\bA=\bc\otimes\bTheta$,
\begin{equation}
 \bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}
 =\bP_{\bc}\otimes\bPi_{\bTheta}^{\perp}+\bPi_{\bc}^{\perp}\otimes\bI,
 \qquad \bP_{\bc}=\frac{\bc\bc^{\mathsf H}}{\|\bc\|^2}.
 \label{eq:kronsplit}
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}

\begin{IEEEproof}
$\bA^{\mathsf H}\bA=(\bc^{\mathsf H}\bc)(\bTheta^{\mathsf H}\bTheta)$ and
$\bA^{\dagger}=\bc^{\dagger}\otimes\bTheta^{\dagger}$, so
$\bP_{\bA}=\bP_{\bc}\otimes\bP_{\bTheta}$. Expanding
$\bI=(\bP_{\bc}+\bPi_{\bc}^{\perp})\otimes(\bP_{\bTheta}
+\bPi_{\bTheta}^{\perp})$ and subtracting $\bP_{\bA}$ gives
\eqref{eq:kronsplit}. No rank assumption is used, so the identity survives
the component mask of C1(ii), which can make the retained Jones block rank
deficient.
\end{IEEEproof}

\begin{theorem}[C1: Maxwell--Jones information splitting]
\label{thm:split}
\textbf{(i) Information consequence.} For one panel, write
$\bA=\bc\otimes\bTheta$, $\boldsymbol{\zeta}=\bTheta\bx$,
$\dot\bc_i=\partial\bc/\partial\chi_i$, and
$\dot{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}_i=(\partial\bTheta/\partial\chi_i)\bx$. Then
\begin{equation}
 [\bD_\chi^{\mathsf H}\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi]_{ij}
 =\|\boldsymbol{\zeta}\|^2\dot\bc_i^{\mathsf H}\bPi_{\bc}^\perp\dot\bc_j
 +\|\bc\|^2\dot{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}_i^{\mathsf H}
 \bPi_{\bTheta}^\perp\dot{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}_j.
 \label{eq:split_short}
\end{equation}
Since calibrated infrastructure gives
$\partial\bTheta/\partial\bchi=\mathbf0$, the second term vanishes. Here and
in \eqref{eq:panelres_short}, $\bTheta=\bTheta_k^{(6)}$ is the \emph{full}
six-component block of \eqref{eq:Theta}, and $\bS_{\rm mode}$ ranges over
the candidate component selections of Section~\ref{sec:system_model}. The
mode retaining $\bS_{\rm mode}$ therefore yields, for a single panel and
whenever the retained signal is nonzero,
\begin{equation}
	\bJ_\chi^{\bS_{\rm mode}}=\alpha_{\bS_{\rm mode}}\bJ_\chi^{\rm full},
	\quad
	{\rm PEB}^{\bS_{\rm mode}}
	=\alpha_{\bS_{\rm mode}}^{-1/2}\,{\rm PEB}^{\rm full},
	\label{eq:c1_scaling}
\end{equation}
with $\alpha_{\bS_{\rm mode}}
=\|\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta\bx\|^2/\|\bTheta\bx\|^2$, the ${\rm PEB}$ identity
requiring in addition $\bJ_\chi^{\rm full}\succ\mathbf0$ so that both bounds
are finite. Both statements are single-panel; across panels the scaling becomes a panel-wise reweighting
rather than a global scalar, as treated after the theorem.

\textbf{(ii) Discrimination consequence.} For this comparison, define
$\bA_k^{(\bS)}(\bchi)=\bc_k(\bchi)\otimes
\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_k^{(6)}$. If a competitor block matches panel $k$'s
space--frequency signature, i.e.
$\bc_{k'}(\bchi')\parallel\bc_k(\bchi)$, its free-Jones residual is
\begin{equation}
 \|\bPi_{\bA_{k'}^{(\bS)}(\bchi')}^\perp
 \bA_k^{(\bS)}(\bchi)\bx_k\|
 =\|\bc_k\|\,\|\bPi_{\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_{k'}^{(6)}}^\perp
 \bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_k^{(6)}\bx_k\|.
 \label{eq:panelres_short}
\end{equation}
If the retained competitor block
$\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_{k'}^{(6)}\in\C^{d_{\rm evs}\times2}$ has full row rank
for $d_{\rm evs}\le2$, this residual is zero. With all six components it is at least
$\|\bc_k\|\|\bTheta_k^{(6)}\bx_k\|\sin\theta_{\min}$, where
$\theta_{\min}$ is the minimum principal angle between the two panel
Maxwell subspaces.
\end{theorem}

\begin{IEEEproof}
(i) A Jacobian column of $\bA\bx=\bc\otimes\boldsymbol{\zeta}$ is
$\bD_i=\dot\bc_i\otimes\boldsymbol{\zeta}
+\bc\otimes\dot{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}_i$. Insert Lemma~\ref{lem:kron} into
$\bD_i^{\mathsf H}\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_j$ and apply the mixed-product
rule. The summand $\bP_{\bc}\otimes\bPi_{\bTheta}^{\perp}$ annihilates
$\dot\bc_i\otimes\boldsymbol{\zeta}$ and
$\bPi_{\bc}^{\perp}\otimes\bI$ annihilates
$\bc\otimes\dot{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}_i$, so every cross term carries a
factor $\bPi_{\bc}^{\perp}\bc=\mathbf0$ or
$\bPi_{\bTheta}^{\perp}\boldsymbol{\zeta}=\mathbf0$; the two surviving
terms are \eqref{eq:split_short}. A mask replaces $\bTheta$ by
$\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta$ and leaves $\bc$ untouched, so with
$\dot{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}_i=\mathbf0$ the whole matrix is multiplied by
$\alpha_{\bS_{\rm mode}}$.

(ii) Apply \eqref{eq:kronsplit} to the competitor
$\bA_{k'}^{(\bS)}(\bchi')$ and evaluate it at
$\bc_k\otimes\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_k^{(6)}\bx_k$. Under
$\bc_{k'}\parallel\bc_k$ the term $\bPi_{\bc_{k'}}^{\perp}\otimes\bI$
annihilates its argument, leaving
$\bP_{\bc_{k'}}\otimes\bPi^{\perp}_{\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_{k'}^{(6)}}$ and
hence \eqref{eq:panelres_short}. If
$\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_{k'}^{(6)}\in\C^{d_{\rm evs}\times2}$ has row rank
$d_{\rm evs}\le2$ its range is all of $\C^{d_{\rm evs}}$ and that projector is
zero. For $d_{\rm evs}=6$, let $\bU$
and $\bV$ be orthonormal bases of the two Maxwell subspaces; from
$\sigma_{\max}(\bU^{\mathsf H}\bV)=\cos\theta_{\min}$ every nonzero
$\bz\in\coll(\bTheta_k^{(6)})$ obeys
$\|\bPi_{\bTheta_{k'}^{(6)}}\bz\|\le\cos\theta_{\min}\|\bz\|$ and therefore
$\|\bPi_{\bTheta_{k'}^{(6)}}^{\perp}\bz\|\ge\sin\theta_{\min}\|\bz\|$.
\end{IEEEproof}

C1 says that polarization enters the geometry of the problem as a scalar.
Component selection cannot rotate the information eigenvectors of a panel:
it can only rescale that panel's contribution by the fraction
$\alpha_{\bS_{\rm mode}}$ of polarization energy it retains, which is
\eqref{eq:c1_scaling}. That scaling does not extend to the multi-panel EFIM,
since $\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}$ built on the full
$\bA=[\bA_1,\ldots,\bA_K]$ factors into per-panel projectors only when the
blocks and their projected tangents are mutually orthogonal. In the
approximately block-orthogonal regime it reads
$\bJ_\chi^{\rm free}\approx\frac{2}{\sigma^2}\sum_k
\|\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_k^{(6)}\bx_k\|^2\bJ_k^{\rm geo}$, with
$[\bJ_k^{\rm geo}]_{ij}=\Real\{\dot\bc_{k,i}^{\mathsf H}\bPi_{\bc_k}^\perp
\dot\bc_{k,j}\}$ selection independent, so each panel keeps a different
fraction of its energy and a receiver choice reweights panels unevenly
instead of rescaling the aggregate. Part (ii) is a statement of a different kind: not a local perturbation but
a wrong-panel hypothesis distance, which vanishes for $d_{\rm evs}\le2$ under the
full-row-rank condition of (ii) while the single-panel information of (i)
merely rescales. Local information and
cross-panel discrimination are therefore separate consequences of receiver
choice, and only the first is visible to a local bound.

\begin{remark}[Scope of the free-Jones reference bound]
\label{rem:peb_reading}
The frozen weight $\lambda_k=1$ does not taper with $\sigma$, so the
free-Jones PEB is a reference for the unpenalized data model and is not
claimed as a proven bound for the penalized estimator; the $\lambda$-sweep of
Section~\ref{subsec:res_c1} measures the gap directly.
\end{remark}

\textit{C. C2---Sufficient Maxwell--Kronecker compression:}
\begin{theorem}[C2: Sufficient Maxwell--Kronecker compression]
\label{thm:compress}
Let $\bU_S=\bI_{NT}\otimes\bQ_S$ be the semi-unitary lifting matrix in the
vectorization of \eqref{eq:obs}, and suppose
$\coll[\bA(\bchi)]\subseteq\coll(\bU_S)$ for all admissible $\bchi$. Write
$\by_c=\bU_S^{\mathsf H}\by$ and
$c_\perp=\|(\bI-\bU_S\bU_S^{\mathsf H})\by\|^2$. Under the matched
white-Gaussian model:
(i) for known $\sigma^2$, $\by_c$ is sufficient for $(\bchi,\bx)$;
(ii) for unknown $\sigma^2$, the pair $(\by_c,c_\perp)$ is sufficient for
$(\bchi,\bx,\sigma^2)$, and $\by_c$ alone still preserves the maximizer in
$(\bchi,\bx)$ of the $\sigma^2$-profiled likelihood, because
\begin{equation}
 \|\by-\bA(\bchi)\bx\|^2
 =\|\by_c-\bU_S^{\mathsf H}\bA(\bchi)\bx\|^2+c_\perp
 \label{eq:isometry_short}
\end{equation}
and $c_\perp$ does not depend on $(\bchi,\bx)$;
(iii) $\by_c$ alone preserves the profiled optimizer, the derivatives of
$\|\by-\bA(\bchi)\bx\|^2$ in $(\bchi,\bx)$ to every order, and the free-Jones
EFIM \eqref{eq:efim_short} exactly;
(iv) the noiseless delay poles are preserved provided the compressed
non-frequency snapshot matrix $[\bw_1,\ldots,\bw_K]$ of
\eqref{eq:freqsnap}, with $\bw_k=\ba_k\otimes\bh_k$ and
$\ba_k=\beta_k\bQ_S^{\mathsf H}\bBm_k\be_k$, has rank $K$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{IEEEproof}
Complete $\bU_S$ to a unitary $[\bU_S,\bU_S^{\perp}]$. Then
$\bU_S^{\mathsf H}\by\sim\mathcal{CN}(\bU_S^{\mathsf H}\bA\bx,\sigma^2\bI)$
and $(\bU_S^{\perp})^{\mathsf H}\by\sim\mathcal{CN}(\mathbf0,\sigma^2\bI)$
independently, the second mean vanishing by the assumed subspace inclusion.
For known $\sigma^2$ the second density does not involve $(\bchi,\bx)$,
which is (i); for unknown $\sigma^2$ it depends on the discarded block only
through the scalar $c_\perp$, so the factorization theorem gives (ii).
Because $\bA=\bU_S\bU_S^{\mathsf H}\bA$, the cross term in the orthogonal
decomposition of $\by$ about $\coll(\bU_S)$ vanishes, which is exactly
\eqref{eq:isometry_short}; its second term is data only. Writing
$\bA=\bU_S\bA_c$ and $\bD_\chi=\bU_S\bD_{\chi,c}$, semi-unitarity gives
$\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi=\bU_S\bPi_{\bA_c}^{\perp}\bD_{\chi,c}$,
pseudoinverses included, and taking the Gram of both sides leaves
\eqref{eq:efim_short} unchanged, proving (iii). For (iv),
$\bQ_S^{\mathsf H}$ acts on the EVS mode alone and therefore commutes with
both Vandermonde frequency factors, so the noiseless pole set is unchanged
and shift-invariance identifiability survives whenever the compressed
snapshot matrix has column rank $K$.
\end{IEEEproof}

C2 separates statistical sufficiency from finite-sample algorithmic
conditioning. Equation~\eqref{eq:isometry_short} is a pointwise identity,
not an approximation, and its discarded term $c_\perp$ carries no
dependence on $(\bchi,\bx)$, so no candidate changes its score relative to
any other and the profiled optimizer and free-Jones EFIM are preserved
exactly; the projection meanwhile removes $I-r$ noise-only receive
directions and cuts residual and Jacobian rows by $I/r$. Phase~I is an
ESPRIT-based subspace procedure rather than the exact ML optimizer, so that
removal can materially improve its finite-sample recovery without
contradicting the isometry. Pole preservation additionally requires
Theorem~\ref{thm:compress}(iv), a rank condition generically expected once
$rT\ge K$ under nondegenerate panel responses and training.

\textit{D. C3---Certified common-clock profiling:}
\begin{theorem}[C3: Certified common-clock profiling]
\label{thm:clock}
Let $\mathcal P$ and $\mathcal T$ be the compact search sets of
Section~\ref{subsec:defchain} and assume
$\bG_\lambda(\bp)=\bG_0(\bp)+\bR_{\rm dir}(\bp)\succ\mathbf0$ on $\mathcal P$, which
for $d_{\rm evs}<2$ requires $\lambda_k>0$.

\textbf{(i) Exact nesting.} Analytic Jones elimination and global inner
clock maximization nest the penalized criterion \eqref{eq:penalized_short}
exactly:
$F(\bp,\Delta t)=\min_{\bx}J(\bp,\Delta t,\bx)=\|\by\|^2-Q(\bp,\Delta t)$,
\begin{equation}
 \min_{\bp\in\mathcal P}\widetilde F(\bp)
 =\min_{\bp\in\mathcal P,\,\Delta t\in\mathcal T,\,\bx\in\C^{2K}}
 J(\bp,\Delta t,\bx),
 \label{eq:nestval_short}
\end{equation}
and the two argument sets correspond, $\arg\min_{\bp}\widetilde F$ being the
projection onto $\bp$ of $\arg\min_{\bp,\Delta t,\bx}J$, so pairing any outer
minimizer $\bp^\star$ with a global inner clock maximizer of
$Q(\bp^\star,\cdot)$ and with
$\bx^\star=\bG_\lambda^{-1}(\bp^\star)\bb(\bp^\star,\Delta t^\star)$ recovers a joint
minimizer.

\textbf{(ii) Unit invariance.} The outer profile is invariant under every
invertible affine clock-unit reparameterization with $\mathcal T$
transformed accordingly.

\textbf{(iii) Certified clock solution.} The interval bound
\eqref{eq:bound_short} guarantees at every visited position
\begin{equation}
 Q(\Delta t^\star)-Q(\hat{\Delta t})\le g_{\rm cert}
 =U-Q(\hat{\Delta t}),
 \label{eq:cert_short}
\end{equation}
and $g_{\rm cert}\le\epsilon_{\rm clk}$ whenever termination occurs by the
prescribed tolerance rather than the subdivision cap.
\end{theorem}

\begin{IEEEproof}
(i) By \eqref{eq:orbit_short} the orbit map $\bU_{\rm clk}$ is unitary, so
$\bG_\lambda=\bA_0^{\mathsf H}\bA_0+\bR_{\rm dir}$ carries no clock. For fixed
$(\bp,\Delta t)$, $J=\|\by\|^2-2\Real\{\bb^{\mathsf H}\bx\}
+\bx^{\mathsf H}\bG_\lambda\bx$ is therefore a strictly convex quadratic in $\bx$
with unique minimizer $\bG_\lambda^{-1}\bb$ and minimum $\|\by\|^2-Q$. The inner
minimum being attained at every $(\bp,\Delta t)$, minimization may be
carried out in any order, which is \eqref{eq:nestval_short}, and the
correspondence of argument sets follows from that attainment. (ii) An
invertible affine change of clock unit is a bijection of $\mathcal T$ onto
its image, so the attained value set of $Q(\bp,\cdot)$, hence
$\widetilde F$, is unchanged. (iii) Termwise differentiation of
\eqref{eq:trig_short} gives $|Q''|\le(2\pi\Delta f)^2
\sum_\ell\ell^2|\gamma_\ell|=L_2$ uniformly on $\mathcal T$, so Taylor's theorem
about an interval center yields \eqref{eq:bound_short}. The solver keeps a
finite cover of $\mathcal T$ and discards an interval only when its upper
bound falls below the incumbent, so no discarded interval can contain a
maximizer and the largest surviving bound obeys $Q(\Delta t^\star)\le U$.
This proves \eqref{eq:cert_short} under any stopping rule, so the gap stays
valid even if the subdivision cap is reached; only the tolerance test
depends on the cap.
\end{IEEEproof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:clock} globalizes only the conditional one-dimensional
clock solve and removes its outer coordinate; it does not certify the outer
three-dimensional position search, which remains local. The corresponding
clock-unit and certificate controls are reported in
Section~\ref{subsec:res_c3}.

\textit{E. Complexity:}
Phase~I is dominated by the $N\times rT$ delay-subspace step and the
factorial first tier of association, and its measured share of the budget is
reported in Section~\ref{subsec:res_cost}. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:compress}, Phase~II evaluates the exact forward model on
the sufficient compressed observation, so compression lowers its cost
without approximating the criterion: each outer position evaluation costs
$O(qM_r+q^2(N+T))+O(q^2N^2)+O(qM_c\log M_c+q^3)+C_{\rm cert}$, with $q=2K$,
$M_r=rNT$ compressed rows in place of the $M=I\,N\,T$ raw rows, $M_c$ the
zero-padded clock-FFT length, and $C_{\rm cert}$ the branch-and-bound
certification cost, which grows with the number of visited intervals. Because the Kronecker structure of
\eqref{eq:Ak} makes each Gram block a cached position-independent
Maxwell--Jones factor times one length-$N$ and one length-$T$ inner
product, the Gram update costs $O(q^2(N+T))$ rather than $O(q^2M_r)$. The
term-by-term derivation is supplementary; measured runtime and memory are
reported in Section~\ref{subsec:res_cost}.
\section{Numerical Results}
\label{sec:simulation}

The study first benchmarks end-to-end performance, then tests C1--C3
separately, and finally examines deployment boundaries and computational
cost.

\subsection{Protocol and Reference Bounds}
\label{subsec:res_setup}

Unless varied, the matched scene uses $f_c=60$~GHz, $K=3$ oriented
$64\times64$ half-wavelength RISs, an $M_A=16$ EVS ULA, $N=63$
subcarriers at $\Delta f=5$~MHz, and $T=256$ training blocks, with the UE at
$(1.25,0.55,0.75)$~m and a true clock of $5$~ns in a $[0,10]$-ns search
interval; complete geometry and signal parameters are supplementary. Path
gains and Jones states are drawn independently per trial, and the signal-to-noise
ratio (SNR) is the average pre-noise sample SNR on the active receive
entries,
${\rm SNR}=10\log_{10}[\|\bmu_{\rm act}\|_2^2/(M_{\rm act}\sigma^2)]$, with
absolute path loss absorbed into this normalization; the receiver-mode
study instead fixes $\sigma^2$ from the full-EVS observation and reuses it
across masks. Phase~I acquires the basin when it returns a finite
$\hat\bp_{\rm I}$ with $\|\hat\bp_{\rm I}-\bp^\star\|_2\le0.1$~m. The
declared operating region is ${\rm SNR}\ge-10$~dB; results quoted at
$-15$ or $-20$~dB are below it and are labeled as such.

Each accuracy cell contains $480$ paired trials, except the external
benchmark of Section~\ref{subsec:res_benchmark}, which uses $960$, the C3
factor control at $100$ and the serialized cost cells at $30$; methods in
one cell share the scene, RIS codes, Jones states, gains and noise
realization, while different suites use independent seed roots. A position
estimate is catastrophic if the method fails or its error exceeds $0.1$~m,
a clock estimate if its absolute error exceeds $1$~ns. Rate intervals are exact $95\%$
Clopper--Pearson, p95 and p99 quoted in the text are nearest-rank order
statistics recomputed from the per-trial columns, paired
differences use bootstrap intervals, and outlier exchanges use McNemar's
exact test; all hyperparameters were fixed before the reported cells were
evaluated. Each fixed-SNR study is measured at $-10$~dB except
the model-order and RIS element-phase sweeps at $0$~dB, and rates from
different suites are never pooled. Phase~I acquires from the beam-space
proposals alone in the benchmark, ablation, compression, receiver and clock
suites, and unions them with the fixed-count dictionary in the
resolvability, position-generalization and resource-scaling sweeps, which
include panels down to $16\times16$ elements
(Section~\ref{subsec:beamspace}).

At $-10$~dB, over $20$ Jones/gain realizations at the default position, the
median free-Jones PEB and CEB are $0.404$~mm and $3.35$~ps; oracle- and
free-Jones PEBs differ by under $0.005\%$, so the
data-only bound is the sole reference, and since the EFIM scales as
$2/\sigma^2$, any bound quoted at another SNR is this median scaled by
$\sigma$. The bound protocol, and the complete sweep behind every study
below, are supplementary.

\subsection{External Benchmark}
\label{subsec:res_benchmark}

\begin{figure*}[!tb]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_benchmark_main.pdf}
\caption{External benchmark, as-published tier, $960$ shared-realization
trials per SNR: the proposed MKSC-GI+CCOP-JVP, 4-D Jones-VP (4D-JVP) in range
units, Algebraic CPD with joint mapping, VBI/SBL at its own closed-form
fusion, and near-field OMP--SAGE--WLS. (a),~(c)~In-basin precision against the
free-Jones PEB and the $\sigma$-scaled CEB reference of
Section~\ref{subsec:res_setup}. (b),~(d)~Complementary CDFs at $-10$~dB; the
dashed lines mark the $0.1$-m and $1$-ns catastrophic thresholds, so each
crossing is that method's catastrophic rate. Catastrophic rates against SNR
and the full campaign range are supplementary.}
\label{fig:benchmark_combined}
\end{figure*}

Five methods run on shared realizations under an as-published protocol
(Fig.~\ref{fig:benchmark_combined}): every route is stopped at the
continuous read-out its own cited formulation prescribes -- algebraic
canonical polyadic decomposition (CPD)~\cite{lin2021tensor} with
alternating-least-squares
polish~\cite{sidiropoulos2017tensor}, read out in the
CP-factor domain; near-field orthogonal matching pursuit
(OMP)~\cite{pati1993omp} through space-alternating generalized
expectation--maximization (SAGE)~\cite{fleury1999sage} and a weighted
least-squares read-out~\cite{yan2025ris}; and variational Bayesian
inference with sparse Bayesian learning (VBI/SBL)~\cite{tipping2001sbl}
through its native closed-form fusion~\cite{li2024vbi} -- and no comparator
receives the proposed route's exact-model Phase~II polish. Every number
below is a property of the evaluated adaptation, not of the reference
method; sources, grids, caps and stopping rules are supplementary.

Against the closest in-model control -- a four-dimensional Jones-VP search
(4D-JVP) that shares the proposed forward model and carries the clock in
range units -- the proposed route attains at $-10$~dB a $0.310$-mm median
position error at a $0.42\%$ catastrophic rate, whereas 4D-JVP matches the
median ($0.312$~mm) but not the tail ($1.98\%$).

The three adapted baselines separate more sharply once each is stopped at
its own read-out. VBI/SBL's closed-form fusion gives a $26.2$-mm median at
a $9.79\%$ catastrophic rate, the Algebraic CPD adaptation is catastrophic on
$46.4\%$ of trials, and near-field OMP is catastrophic in all $960$ trials
with a $1.2$-m median. None improves with SNR, identifying these as read-out
or acquisition floors rather than noise-limited behavior: over $-5$ to $30$~dB the VBI/SBL median stays within $26.10$--$26.13$~mm,
the Algebraic CPD rate within $33.6$--$39.4\%$, and near-field OMP above
$99.8\%$; for near-field OMP the far-field
direction-acquisition stage fails to bracket a basin in this blocked-LoS,
deep-near-field scene.

McNemar's exact test on the paired catastrophic indicator rejects equality
against every comparator ($p\le1.5\times10^{-3}$), on $21$ exchanges
against 4D-JVP and $98$, $441$ and $956$ against VBI/SBL,
Algebraic CPD and near-field OMP, the last two entirely one-sided. Against
the same-forward-model control the advantage is confined to acquisition:
from $-5$~dB upward the proposed route has no catastrophic trial, matched by
4D-JVP only from $0$~dB.

Synchronization mirrors the position ranking: at $-10$~dB the
clock-catastrophic rates are $0.21\%$ for the proposed route against
$1.56\%$, $7.29\%$, $7.92\%$ and $89.7\%$ for 4D-JVP,
Algebraic CPD, VBI/SBL and near-field OMP. At $0$~dB the proposed clock RMSE
($1.08$~ps) sits at a $1.06$-ps CEB reference, a descriptive comparison and
not a bound test (Remark~\ref{rem:peb_reading}), and 4D-JVP in range units
matches that inlier precision; the adapted baselines instead carry
$8.1\times10^{-3}$--$0.11$ normalized mean-square error among their own
inliers, a property of the geometries they acquire. The ordering survives
outside the declared region: at $-15$ and $-20$~dB the proposed route keeps
catastrophic rates of $1.25\%$ and $5.00\%$ against $6.88\%$ and $22.08\%$
for 4D-JVP, the lowest of any evaluated route at both
points.

A refinement-matched audit, on an independent seed root, feeds each
tier-sensitive acquisition into the same exact-model refinement -- a common
diagnostic map, not a stage of any cited method. VBI/SBL then reaches a
$0.315$-mm median at $0.52\%$ against $0.316$~mm and $0.31\%$ for the
proposed route, with matching median reconstruction and clock errors and
McNemar not rejecting equality ($p=0.73$): its $26$-mm floor is a property
of the adapted read-out, not of the sparse Bayesian acquisition. Algebraic
CPD recovers comparable in-basin precision ($0.438$ against $0.453$~mm
conditional RMSE) yet keeps a $41.9\%$ catastrophic rate, so its loss lies
upstream of refinement rather than in the read-out this tier replaces. The
archived per-trial diagnostics narrow it further: on the failing trials the
tensor fit is as accurate as on the succeeding ones and the panel
association is correct in every case, leaving the map from converged CP
factors to position as the remaining explanation (supplement). Near-field OMP--SAGE--WLS is
tier-invariant, the polish it runs being the SAGE stage its own reference
prescribes, and is unchanged. Rates are paired within a tier only.

\begin{figure}[!tb]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_receiver_main.pdf}
\caption{C1 information, $480$ paired trials per cell at a common absolute
noise level: matched conditional accuracy (solid) against the free-Jones PEB
(dashed), the modes ordering as Theorem~\ref{thm:split}(i) predicts. The
paired efficiency ratio and its bootstrap intervals are supplementary.}
\label{fig:c1_receiver}
\end{figure}

\subsection{C1: EVS Information and Panel Separability}
\label{subsec:res_c1}

\textit{Information.} Theorem~\ref{thm:split}(i) is an identity, so what
the receiver-mode experiment measures is the price of its scalar weight: at
$0$~dB the scalar, dual and full modes reach conditional RMSEs of $0.815$,
$0.330$ and $0.133$~mm, in the order the matched PEB predicts
(Fig.~\ref{fig:c1_receiver}). The identity itself reproduces
$\bJ_\chi^{\bS_{\rm mode}}=\alpha_{\bS_{\rm mode}}\bJ_\chi^{\rm full}$
numerically to $5.7\times10^{-16}$.

\textit{Separability.} Discrimination changes by more than information
alone predicts. At $-10$~dB the three modes carry catastrophic rates of
$14.17\%$, $2.29\%$ and $1.04\%$ -- a $13.6\times$ scalar/full ratio
against a $5.7\times$ matched-PEB ratio -- and the excess is the
panel-label loss of Theorem~\ref{thm:split}(ii).
Fig.~\ref{fig:c1_separability} isolates that mechanism on the same $K=3$
scene with the cascaded-delay separation and the Jones overlap of the
closest-delay panel pair set directly. Its resolution criterion (caption)
excludes position accuracy, so the curve measures separation of the
components and not the accuracy of the fix built on them. At $1$~ns
cascaded-delay separation, full EVS meets that criterion with probability
$0.87$--$0.88$ across the four tested Jones overlaps: their largest spread
is $1.7$ percentage points, below the $4.6$-point Clopper--Pearson
half-width of a single cell, so the sweep resolves no dependence on
overlap. The dual-polarized and scalar modes instead collapse toward zero
as the overlap grows, spanning $82.7$ and $61.9$ points. Formal trend tests
over overlap are supplementary.
This is \eqref{eq:panelres_short} in the data: with $d_{\rm evs}\le2$ retained
components the competing Jones block spans $\C^{d}$ and the residual
separating the two panels can vanish outright, whereas six components hold
it above $\sin\theta_{\min}$. Delay separation limits every mode
alike; only the reduced modes are additionally limited by polarization.

\textit{Efficiency.} Inside the operating region the full-EVS RMSE/PEB
ratio is $0.982$--$0.992$ in the receiver suite and $1.017$--$1.020$ in the
independently seeded internal suite, and the dual-polarized ratio is
$1.057$--$1.084$. At $480$ trials the paired-bootstrap intervals are
$\pm6\%$ and $\pm9\%$ (supplement), so both modes are
consistent with efficiency while no ordering between them is resolved.
Their separation from scalar sensing in Fig.~\ref{fig:c1_receiver}
tracks the SNR-independent PEB ratios $5.7$ and $2.3$. The scalar mode's
high-SNR floor (near $0.098$~mm at $30$~dB) is receiver rather than
regularizer driven, and we attribute it to the rank-one structure of
single-component sensing. A penalty sweep over
$\lambda_k\in\{0,0.1,1,10\}$ in all three modes exchanges no catastrophic
trial and moves inlier RMSE by at most $2$ parts in $10^5$, the attained
residual staying identical to machine precision. That invariance is the one
Section~\ref{subsec:ccop} predicts: in the reference geometry the scalar mask
drops $\rank\bG_0$ from $2K$ to $K$ and the dual mask leaves
$\cond\bG_0\sim10^3$ against $1.1$ at full EVS, and $\bR_{\rm dir}$ acts
only in the directions those deficiencies make invisible.

\begin{figure*}[!tb]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.80\textwidth]{fig_resolvability.pdf}
\caption{Polarimetric panel separability at $-10$~dB, the mechanism of
Theorem~\ref{thm:split}(ii): resolution probability -- the fraction of
trials returning $K$ correctly paired components with Phase-I delay error at
most $0.5$~ns and no pole collapse -- against cascaded-delay
separation at four Jones overlaps with exact Clopper--Pearson bands, for
(a)~scalar, (b)~dual-polarized and (c)~full-EVS reception. The dashed
vertical line marks the reference scene's minimum separation and the boxed
number the largest spread over overlap.}
\label{fig:c1_separability}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{C2: Sufficient Compression and Reliable Acquisition}
\label{subsec:res_c2}

\textit{Effect of sufficient compression on acquisition reliability.}
Theorem~\ref{thm:compress} establishes that the projection preserves the
profiled maximizer and the free-Jones information, so what the experiment
asks is what an exactly sufficient projection does to the finite-sample
ESPRIT front end; from $0$~dB upward the compressed and raw routes are
numerically coincident, that finite-sample advantage having saturated. At
$-10$~dB the leakage
ratio $\sigma_{K+1}/\sigma_K$ falls from $0.822$ to $0.353$, the delay p95
error improves $35\times$ ($4.85\to0.140$~ns), basin acquisition rises from
$97.50\%$ to $99.38\%$ and the catastrophic position rate falls from
$2.29\%$ to $0.62\%$. Exact-model accuracy is therefore
unaltered; what the identity permits is discarding $90$ of $96$ receive
dimensions before a subspace method is applied to the remainder.

\textit{Contribution of the individual Phase~I mechanisms.} Table~\ref{tab:c2_ladder}
separates the three Phase~I mechanisms on a nested ladder at $-20$~dB, a
stress point below the declared operating region chosen because the tail
there is large enough to resolve them. Compression alone removes three
quarters of the
catastrophic mass ($21.46\to5.62\%$) by denoising the delay poles;
common-geometry fusion, by turning $K$ disagreeing panel-local fixes into
one position, is what converts that into basin capture
($78.12\to96.04\%$); the additional deterministic starts recover only two
further trials; and the anchor refresh leaves both the rate and the basin
untouched, its evidence being the p95 reconstruction NMSE, which it lowers
$27$-fold from $2.6\times10^{-2}$ to $9.6\times10^{-4}$. None of this is
restart diversity: a seven-start pool and an oracle-position start both land
within one trial of the proposed rung.

\textit{Aperture scaling.} Growing the panel
from $16^2$ to $64^2$ elements lowers conditional RMSE from $1.66$ to
$0.42$~mm while holding the catastrophic rate in $[0.21,0.62]\%$, so the
estimator realizes the aperture gain that motivates near-field operation.
Collecting it is an acquisition property, not a refinement property: the
proposed Phase~I holds a basin rate of $99.2$--$99.6\%$ across that
sixteenfold change in element count, whereas the baseline Phase~I ranges
from $14.58\%$ at $M_R=16^2$ to $95.62\%$ at $64^2$ and never reaches the
proposed rate at any tested aperture. Accuracy improves monotonically with
$M_A$, $T$ and bandwidth, and at $K=4$ the proposed route and 4D-JVP carry
catastrophic rates of $1.88\%$ and $12.92\%$.

