\subsection{C3: Clock Profiling and Certification}
\label{subsec:res_c3}

Table~\ref{tab:c3_control} isolates Phase~II at $-10$~dB by giving four
configurations the same $100$ MKSC-GI initializers. CCOP-JVP matches the precision
of 4D-JVP while removing one nonlinear coordinate and cutting the median
outer evaluation count from $13$ to $9$, and it eliminates sensitivity to
the clock coordinate's units: the explicit search depends on whether the
clock is carried in seconds, nanoseconds or range units --
the seconds label degrading conditional position RMSE by $31\%$ -- whereas
CCOP-JVP returns bit-identical output under all three parameterizations, as
Theorem~\ref{thm:clock}(ii) requires.

The certification condition is satisfied throughout the tested operating
region. In the independently seeded internal suite, every one of the
$35\,833$ clock profiles solved across its $4\,320$ operating-region trials
returned a gap below its tolerance; taking the worst profile call visited
within each trial, the gap normalized by that tolerance has median $0.87$
and maximum $0.99999$, so the subdivision cap was never reached. The
per-position read-out and the outer-search diagnostics are supplementary.
The certificate covers the inner solve only, and indeed 4D-JVP and CCOP-JVP
sharing one Phase~I
initializer have identical catastrophic rates at every SNR of the
independent suite, which is why the tail suppression of
Section~\ref{subsec:res_c2} is attributed to C2 and not to C3.

