\subsection{Deployment Boundaries and Spatial Generalization}
\label{subsec:res_robustness}

Three sensitivities set the deployment envelope, two of them in
Fig.~\ref{fig:boundaries}; the model-order panel, the remaining mismatch and
generalization sweeps and the audit of the frozen Phase~I constants are
supplementary.
\textit{Calibration} is the binding one
(Fig.~\ref{fig:boundaries}(a)--(b)): RIS--BS angular error degrades
precision long before capture,
raising conditional RMSE from $0.447$ to $31.2$~mm at $0.25^\circ$ while
the basin rate holds at $99.17\%$, and capture itself collapses only at
$1^\circ$ ($59.58\%$). Receiver gain, phase and sensor-position mismatch,
RIS element-phase error and first-order colored noise are all milder over
their tested ranges.

\textit{Model order} is a material assumption rather than a tuning
parameter. In the dedicated $0$-dB sweep, $\widehat K=2$ is structurally
destructive ($0.710$-m median, $P_{\rm cat}=65.4\%$), and $\widehat K=4$
and $5$ keep submillimeter medians while carrying $35.4\%$ and $49.0\%$
catastrophic rates.

\textit{Delay coincidence} bounds acquisition where information does not
(Fig.~\ref{fig:boundaries}(c)). Across a $5\times5\times2$ position grid the proposed median per-position
catastrophic rate is $0.62\%$ against $2.92\%$ for 4D-JVP, and both hard
corners lie near a cascaded-delay coincidence surface, the worst reaching
$31.25\%$ at a minimum separation of $0.062/B_{\rm occ}$ -- while the
free-Jones EFIM stays full rank at every tested position, corners included.
Local information sufficiency therefore does not imply global acquisition
reliability: deployment screening needs a delay-coincidence check and a
coarse global fallback, not a PEB map alone. Every sweep here holds one
infrastructure layout and samples no panel-placement diversity.

\subsection{Cost, Training Budget, and Failure Diagnostics}
\label{subsec:res_cost}

\textit{Cost.} In the serialized single-thread benchmark at $-10$~dB the
proposed route requires $3.20$~s and $2.52$~GB peak resident set size on
the $96$ calibrated receive chains a full EVS costs:
faster than Algebraic CPD ($4.51$~s) and near-field OMP--SAGE--WLS
($11.5$~s), slower than 4D-JVP ($2.42$~s) and VBI/SBL's native
read-out ($1.06$~s), the fastest tier but the one that pays the floor of
Section~\ref{subsec:res_benchmark}; all timings share one host, absolute
seconds not being portable. An independently seeded $30$-trial
decomposition agrees with that total to under $1\%$ and places $2.86$~s of
it in Phase~I against $0.33$~s in certified clock profiling, so acquisition
and not refinement dominates the budget. The per-block breakdown and
per-method memory are supplementary.

\textit{Training and mobility.} The training budget is set by mobility
rather than by bandwidth. With
$\Delta f=5$~MHz a $25\%$ cyclic prefix is $50$~ns, covering the $19.1$-ns
sum of cascaded-delay spread and clock-search interval, so one
configuration per symbol gives $T_{\rm train}\simeq64~\mu$s at $T=256$,
excluding a reconfiguration latency that dominates the payload beyond about
$0.25~\mu$s per switch. At $1$~m/s the UE moves $64~\mu$m per sweep, $16\%$
of the median PEB, the two becoming equal near $6$~m/s. This is a displacement check against a
static bound, not a time-varying channel experiment.

\textit{Failure localization.} Across the $4\,320$ operating-region trials,
exactly five proposed-route trials are catastrophic, and exactly those five
fail the basin diagnostic, each with a Phase~I delay RMSE above $0.5$~ns;
panel association is correct in $99.93\%$ of trials overall and in every
trial at and above $0$~dB. Every failure in the tested region therefore
originates in acquisition rather than in refinement.
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We studied joint 3-D localization and synchronization in a blocked-LoS
distributed near-field RIS uplink observed by one shared EVS array, and
showed that a single algebraic fact organizes it: the per-panel sensing block
is a Kronecker product of a space--frequency factor and a calibrated
Maxwell--Jones factor, one consequence for each difficulty the architecture
creates. Its projector splits, so polarization enters the
information as a scalar weight while panel separability obeys a separate
condition a reduced receiver can void outright (C1). Its calibrated factor
confines every response to a $2K$-dimensional receive subspace, making the
$96\to6$ projection lossless for the profiled maximizer and the
free-Jones information (C2). Its clock dependence is one unitary orbit,
turning the conditional profile into a finite trigonometric polynomial solved
to a deterministic optimality-gap certificate and removing a dimension from
the outer search (C3).

Empirically the gain is concentrated in basin reliability rather than in
local precision. The proposed route and 4D-JVP agree to within $1\%$ once they
reach the same basin; what separates them is the catastrophic tail, and every
catastrophic trial in the operating-region suite fails the Phase-I basin
diagnostic. The tail is therefore a property of acquisition -- EVS
discrimination, compressed delay recovery, panel association and
common-geometry fusion -- not of refinement: sharing one initializer, the two
refinement routes return identical catastrophic rates across the independent
suite. CCOP-JVP contributes a lower-dimensional, unit-invariant refinement
with a certified inner solve, not a global outer search.

Local information is not what fails first here: retaining fewer Maxwell
components rescales a panel's information by a known scalar yet can drive to
zero the residual separating one panel from another, and the free-Jones EFIM
stays full rank even at the swept positions whose catastrophic rate reaches
$31\%$ near a cascaded-delay coincidence surface. What degrades first is
discriminability -- of panels from one another, and of the correct basin from
its competitors -- which a position-error bound cannot see. Receiver-mode
selection and deployment screening should therefore rest on the
Maxwell-subspace principal angle and the cascaded-delay separation, not on a
bound map alone.

Three boundaries remain within the model: RIS--BS angular calibration limits
millimeter accuracy well before it limits capture; the panel count is a
material model-order assumption; and the factorial first tier of panel
association, negligible at $K=3$, binds as panels are added. Spatial-wideband
effects, direct leakage and diffuse multipath, and panel-placement diversity
lie outside it. The two nontrivial openings are a polynomial-time association
surrogate and coarse global screening of coincidence regions.

